Yes. I’ve made this a high-priority audit lane, and the key addition is that it explicitly audits the **diagnosis itself**.

So we now distinguish:

- a genuine SAT finding,
- a supposed finding that was never actually established,
- a genuine failure of the specific formulation tested,
- and a supposed failure that comes from a bad critique, wrong target, dimensional misunderstanding, provenance mistake, symbol collision, status mistake, etc.

I also encoded the scope rule you’ve emphasized repeatedly: **one failed implementation is evidence that that implementation failed, not that every possible formulation of the idea failed.** The same goes in reverse: one successful toy case does not establish the whole theory.

There is now a separate **AUDITOR ERROR / META-AUDIT FAILURE** class for things like:
- calling \(Z_3\) hand-inserted when a branch actually derives it;
- throwing out the \(H_0+c\) concept because the shorthand is dimensionally wrong rather than testing a repaired formulation;
- treating reduced-representation phase loss as proof that the full torus is phase-lossy;
- treating a failed code prototype as impossibility proof;
- confusing similarly valued constants;
- mistaking historical lattice machinery for mandatory current ontology;
- excluding assistant-originated GLASS hypotheses from SAT history;
- or treating a speculative branch failure as a failure of SAT core.

The audit is deliberately symmetric: real criticisms should survive and become part of the reconstruction just as firmly as real successes.

I also specified an eventual **Error / Findings / Failure Ledger**, with source, claim, historical stage, original diagnosis, reproduced audit result, error class, scope, correction, superseding formulation, and unresolved questions.

[Error / Findings / Failure Meta-Audit Priority](https://github.com/Satobloc/HsH/blob/main/WORKSPACES/COMMON/PRIORITY_ERROR_FINDING_AND_META_AUDIT_2026-09-18.md)

